Establish Stakeholder Roles and Responsibilities



during assessment or audit preparation, stakeholders are identified along with their respective roles and responsibilities


stakeholders in this context include anyone involved in the assessment, anyone affected by the assessment (e.g. operational impacts due to disruptions to normal activities), or anyone who will use the output of the assessment (e.g. board of directors, external customers). stakeholders might not play and active part in the assessment process, yet they could set goals or objectives that need to be achieved.


for example, under the NIST RMF, the Authorizing Official (AO) is the primary stakeholder who will use the assessment report to make a risk based decision regarding a system under assessment. the AO is typically a senior official with the authority to formally assume responsibility for operating a system at an acceptable level of risk. this role is critical to ensuring the systems under their purview adhere to required security standards and practices, safeguarding the integrity, confidentiality, and availability of information. within the RMF, the AO is responsible for assigning the assessment team and determining appropriate levels of independence and technical competence for the assessment team. the AO also has the responsibility to review and approve the assessment plan prior to the assessment. ISO and many other frameworks do not explicitly define stakeholder roles, leaving the identification and role responsibilities up to the organization to define.


The Assessment Team: one of the most important roles to identify that has significant responsibilities to the assessment is the role of the assessor or auditor. this is the person or team responsible for planning, conducting, and reporting the assessment outcome. during assessor selection, the assessor or assessment team is chosen for the specific organization or system to be assessed. this may involve anything from a single assessor to a large team with varied skills and experience sets depending on the size and complexity of the assessment


Technical Expertise: one aspect of choosing the correct assessor is to consider their technical expertise and their knowledge of security risk management, hardware, software, or firmware associated with the assessment. assessment teams may be chosen with the skills needed f0or the scope of the assessment. if an assessment team does not possess the correct skill set and technical expertise for the specific assessment, this can significantly affect the quality of the assessment. for standardized audits, a standing or permanent team is often feasible since the items being audited remain consistent across multiple audits. however, when each assessment may contain completely different technologies or system elements (like with NIST RMF), assessment teams may not need to be assign3ed based on the specific contents of each assessment to ensure appropriate expertise is available for the specific assessment.


Independence: the other main factor to consider is the degree of independence the assessor has from the system owner or organization. in this context, independence means there is no conflict of interest for the assessor or audit in the conduct of the assessment or audit, which allows stakeholders to trust the results. if a conflict of interest exists, it can reduce the value of the results. in an ISO certification audit, the auditor is always a third party certification organization, while with RMF assessment there is more leeway in determining the appropriate level of independence of the assessment team. the assessment process can be centrally managed (e.g. through the internal audit department), or the responsibility can be distributed across the organization for the RMF. however, if it is distributed, the appropriate level of independence from conflicts of interest must be maintained. in the NIST RMF, the AO determines the appropriate level of independence required for security control assessment based on organizational policy and guidance.


