Outlining Logistics in a Assessment Plan



once identified, the assessor or auditor typically develops the assessment plan based on input from other stakeholder. much of the planning involves the logistics required to conduct and assessment that will meet the objectives within the time required.


plans developed by assessors are based on Security and Privacy Plans, program management documentation, and common control documentation. for the NIST RMF, these plans are reviewed and approved by the authorizing official to ensure they are consistent with security and privacy objectives and that they employ appropriate procedures, methods, techniques, tools, and automation. appropriate control effectiveness is determined by the plan, which means that not only does the assessment meet to identify that a control is in place but also that it is working effectively to produce the desired results


the items below are elements of the preparatory activities and actions required before constructing the assessment plan. these items are used to develop a comprehensive assessment plan. the lists below include examples of typical preparatory activities and typical assessment plan elements, but organizational guidance and the system under assessment will dictate the specific needs for that assessment. 

preparatory activities: developing a general understanding of the organizations mission, business, and operations. providing information system support to the organizations operations. identifying the general structure and components of the system. identifying organizational entities associated with common controls. ensuring common understand of assessment objectives and scope. obtaining necessary documents and artifacts. establishing points of contact needed for the assessment. obtaining previous assessment results. developing detailed security and privacy plans. prioritizing and scheduling assessments. 

the assessment plan: establishes objective and scope assessment. reviews previous recommendations. allocates necessary resources to carry out the assessment. establishes time frames and key milestones of the assessment. identifies documents and artifacts to be collected and provided to the assessor. includes a communication plan to minimize ambiguities and misunderstandings. includes detailed assessment procedures and expected results. is tailored to the specific scope and objectives. lists tools and resources required. specifies locations and logistics. outlines data handling procedures and protection requirements. addresses legal considerations. establishes a post assessment follow up plan. 


objectives: the assessment objective is what the organization wants to obtain may have multiple objectives, some of which may be obtained from different stakeholders. in other cases, an assessment may have only a single objective (e.g. compliance audit). the objective is the reason for conducting the assessment and results in value for the organization. 


schedule: scheduling ensures resources are available at the time necessary to conduct the assessment and minimizes negative impact to resources used. this impact includes staff being unavailable for their normal duties, disruptions to normal operations, and the use of technologies that may have performance impact or technical impact on operations (e.g. running a test that results in system and network shutdowns).


scope: scoping specifically identified what is and is not part of the assessment. assessments may focus on specific systems, and entire organizations security program (e.g. ISO 270001), or elements of a larger organization or technical infrastructure. the assessment scope defines the parts, pieces, components, processes, technology, and staff included. 


deliverables: deliverables are defined, and appropriate formats are set before the assessment. deliverables must include the necessary elements to meet the objectives defined for the assessment. formats for deliverables are defined to ensure all stakeholder needs will be met by the output of the assessment. 


resources: resources are those, both human and technological, that are available for the assessment. the objectives must be accomplished within the bounds of the available resources and schedule limitations. resource limitations are often driven by the assessment scope, the cost of using the resources, or the operational impact of using the resources.


logistics: logistics can be one of the more complex elements of assessment planning. assessment resources and time (scheduling) will always be limited due to numerous factors. if the relevant people and technology are not available and accessible at the right time, the assessment objectives may not be met. logistics ensure that assessors have appropriate access to facilities and that interview subjects are available at the times specified in the schedule.


