Scoping Assets, Methods, and Level of Effort



inherently, assessments will always be limited by the time and reports available.


before conducting an assessment, a detailed plan is developed that identifies specific goals to achieve the overall objectives and assessment procedures. within each objective, one or more assessment objects have one or more assessment methods applied to determine whether the objective has been met. since time and resources are always limited, to some degree, the plan must balance the level of effort available to conduct the assessment and the level of effort required to achieve acceptable levels of assurance that each objective is met. 


the figure that follow shows the basic components of assessment plan as define within the NIST documentatio9n. ISO uses a similar approach but slightly different terminology to describe the object and methods. the plan has a set of assessment procedures made up of one or more assessment objectives., each of these objectives include assessment of one or more objects, and the assessments use one or more assessment methods for each selected control enhancement.


Object AIMS: 1. activity 2. individual 3. mechanism 4. specification.


methods TIE: 1. test 2. interview 3. examine 


security control classes MOT: 1. management 2. operations 3. technical


findings: 1. satisfied (s) 2. other than satisfied (o)
 


assessment objectives: the following list is the set of NIST defined assessment objects: 

specifications: document based artifacts including policies, procedures, plans, and system security and privacy requirements

mechanisms: hardware, software, or firmware safeguards and countermeasures

activities: system operations, management, exercises, admini8strastion, and processes

individual: people who apply the specifications, mechanisms, or activities


assessment methods: the following list is the seg of NIST defines assessment methods and the types of objects they would be used to assess:

test: exercising assessment objects under specified conditions to compare actual and expected behavior. tests can be applied to mechanisms and activities. 

interview: conductions discussions and asking questions. interviews are applied to individuals or groups of individuals. 

examine: checking, inspecting, reviewing, observing, studying, or analyzing. examination can be applied to specifications, mechanisms, and activities



assessment depth coverage: assessment activities warrant various levels of effort and completeness based on their impact in assessing control effectiveness. the main factors include depth: depth addresses the rigor and level of detail in the assessment methods (e.g. basic: high level, focused: in-depth. comprehensive: detailed and thorough). coverage: coverage addresses the scope or breadth of the examination process (e.g. basic: a representative sample of asse3sment objectives, focused: a representative sample and other specific assessment objectives), comprehensive: all of a sufficiently large sample of assessment objects.



