Auditing Evidence for Demonstration of Compliance


during assessment planning, enough information about the organization, systems, and staff must be available to the assessor to create an effective assessment plan.


this can include prior assessments, documentation, polices, or organizational structure. any information obtained during assessment preparation can be used to better plan the assessment and identify appropriate methods and objects within the assessment plan

objects can take many forms, from pure documentation to specific staff members

many potential objects can be obtained prior to conducting the assessment that may facilitate assessment planning. for example, the figure show that the audit and accountability policy is a potential object to examine. since this is a policy, it should be obtained as early in planning as possible, as the policy itself may help define the objects and methods that will be included in the assessment. for example, the audit policy likely includes information about tother objects that will require examination, staff roles to interview, and technology to test. 



AU7: Audit Reduction and Report Generation

assessment objective: determine whether the information system provides an audit reduction and report generation capability that supports: AU7(a)(1) on demand audit review, AU7(a)(2) analysis, AU7(a)(3) reporting requirements, AU7(a)(4) after the fact investigations of security incidents. AU7(b) does not alter the original content or time ordering of audit records


while the initial evidence collection may begin during the preparation phase, it typically continues through the end of the audit or assessment. the initial evidence is more typically in the form of prior audits or assessments, documentation, diagrams, policy, and seven organization charts.

this all helps in the assessment preparation and is also used as evidence detailing the state of the audit or assessment in the final report. for example, during preparation, the auditor may obtain a policy or document, which informs planning for interviews and technical tests. when conducting the audit, interview notes and test results are added as additional evidence. later, during report preparation, the results may be compared with the obtained policy to determine not only whether the policy is valid but also that it is followed by individuals and implemented on systems. the total body of evidence collected during preparation and audit can be extensive, depending on the audits scope



