Finalizing the Assessment/Audit Plan



in conjunction with appropriate stakeholders, the assessor finalizes the assessment plan.


this can include validating the assessment, objectives, scope, resource availability, schedule, deliverables, and any logistics related to conducting the assessment. planning a complex assessment and plan finalization may take several months an is often an involved in process for multiple stakeholder. while the assessor is typically assigned primary responsibility for planning, they must coordinate assessment impacts with appropriate stakeholders to ensure the assessment can be undertaken within the sources and schedule constrains. for example, in the NIST RMF, finalization of the assessment plan does include and explicit approval but the AO (as the primary stakeholder) that the plan is sufficient and will meet the AOs needs to make a risk decision. while an explicit approval may not be require in all assessment frameworks, many do include4 coordination with stakeholders during plan finalization to ensure the assessment will achieve its intended objectives


small failures and deviations that might occur during the assessment can easily spiral out of control, resulting in assessment objectives not being met

for example, a complex assessment might be scheduled to be conducted at a particular location over five days with interviews and technical assessments fully scheduled across that time. a small logistical failure where the assessment team is not registered for building access might generate a delay in the start time on day one. this can result in schedule compression for the remainder of the were or cause an extension of the intended schedule. by itself, the delay may be inconsequential, but if compounded by similar issues, it can easily result in objectives going unmet by day five. if the schedule is already completely full, even a small reduction in time available can easily result in a lower quality of assessment or in unplanned items begin dropped for lack of time. preplanning and validation of the plan are essential to ensure a smooth, well executed assessment where all objectives are met. 


no assessment plan will be perfect, and all assessment plans should include provisions for addressing issues or roadblocks as they occur. however, due to the complexity in larger assessments, issues will almost certainly occur during the assessment that will affect resources or scheduling

the more accurate the plan is, the less likely the issues are to impact the result. poorly planned assessments are less likely to recover from issues that invariably arise during the assessment.



