Penetration Testing



the assessment plan should detail the methods (e.g. test, interview, examine) and objects (e.g. activities, individuals, mechanisms, specification) that will be used during the assessment.


there are often multiple methods and objects specified for each assessment objective to ensure the objectives have been met by the system or organization being assessed. GRC professionals can apply various methods to the objects based on the objectives, scope, and schedule for the assessment. these methods must be documented, and the documentation techniques should be appropriate to the objective and purpose of the assessment. most compliance assessments and audits typically focus on non adversarial techniques to determine whether compliance objectives are being met. however, a combination of adversarial and non adversarial techniques can be applied to generate the appropriate level of confidence in the results of the assessment.


adversarial techniques simulate or emulate adversarial actions (attacks) and can be used to assess control effectiveness. adversarial techniques are not typically applied during RMF or ISO assessments, but they may be useful to determine control effectiveness from the perspective of a particular threat or attack vector. they can also be used as additional evidence of effectiveness (e.g. in a threat risk assessment). adversarial techniques may include invasive elements. because they can incur the risk of damage to running systems, they require careful planning to prevent system damage or function loss. some adversarial techniques include password cracking and social engineering. password cracking tools and techniques can be used to validate password strength requirements. typical password cracking evaluations attempt to crack passwords by trying many common or simple passwords; identification of low strength passwords within a specified period may indicate a failure. social engineering attempts to trick someone into revealing information or performing an action. although an active social engineering assessment is not typically part of an RMF assessment but rather part of a red teaming exercise, the RMF assessment often includes the evaluation of control intended to prevent social engineering or mitigate its potential effect. many of these controls involve humans and processes, and they may also include some technical elements employed within systems. 


penetration testing: penetration testing (also known as "pen testing") is introduced here as it does assist in verifying the effectiveness of security controls; however, it is typically not used during ISO certification or a NIST RMF assessment as the only assessment methodology. it is not effective in assessing the effectiveness of all controls directly; it can provide a viewpoint only of the overall effectiveness across numerous controls at the time of the assessment. vulnerability scanning or more invasive penetration testing might be used to supplement other assessment methodologies in many assessment types. penetration testing is a special type of testing that takes an adversarial approach by mimicking real world attacks. the resting may be overt or covert. in overt tests, the system or network, operators are aware the penetration testing is occurring. in covert tests, the organization is aware of the testing, but the specific system or network operators are not, which allows the test to encompass operator responses. 


penetration testing is used top determine how well the system tolerates realistic attacks, the level of sophistication required to successfully compromise the system, and additional countermeasure needed to mitigate threats.


in the planning phase, rules are identified, management approval is finalized and documented, and the testing scope, boundaries, and goals are set. the planning phase lays the groundwork for a successful penetration test. no actual testing occurs in this phase. the discovery phase of penetration testing includes two parts. the first part is the start of actual testing and covers information gathering and scanning. network port and service identification is conducted to identify potential targets. the second part of the discovery phase is vulnerability analysis, which involves comparing the services, applications, and operating systems of scanned hosts against vulnerability databases (an automatic process for vulnerability scanners) and the testers own knowledge of vulnerabilities. human testers can use their own databases or public databases such as the National Vulnerability Database (NVD) to identify vulnerabilities manually


