Attack Phase Processes and Reporting


exploitation an attack is at the heart of any penetration test


attack phase process: discovery phase, gaining access, escalating privilege, system browsing, install additional tools

in the event an attack on a specific vulnerability proves impossible, the tester should attempt to exploit another discovered vulnerability, they can install more tools on the target system or network to facilitate the testing process. these tools are used to gain access to additional systems or resources on the network and to obtain access to information about the network or organization. testing and analysis on multiple systems should be conducted during a penetration test to determine the level of access an adversary could gain. this process is represented in the feedback loop between the attack and discovery phase of the penetration test. prior to the attack phase during a penetration test, obtaining explicit approval from the system owner is mandatory. the approval may already be included within the original test authorization but should be validated prior to active attack measures being employed



reporting: once discovery and attack phases are completed, the penetration tester will develop a report. the report should include all activities conducted by the tester, along with specific notes on what was successful as well as what was attempted but unsuccessful. in some cases, the list of attempted discovery or attack methods that failed can be as important as the ones that did not. obviously, in the case of a successful attack, something needs to be corrected. however, proof of an unsuccessful attack can be used as evidence regarding the potential effectiveness of security controls. this must be carefully considered and done with caution, since penetration tests will not exhaustively test controls, but it can often be useful as added confirmation of a control effectiveness against specific attack vectors.


