Compliance Assessments and Audits: Non Adversarial Techniques


non adversarial techniques come in many forms, but all involve the cooperation of the assessment team and the organization being assessed.


this collaborative approach extends to the discovery and scanning phases, where the team systematically identifies and catalogues system and vulnerabilities with the organizations knowledge and consent. it also includes a control assessment, which evaluates the effectiveness of the organizations security measures in a cooperative manner. some examples of common non adversarial techniques are explores in the following sections.


discovery and scanning: scanning techniques are used to observe and report the status of network connected systems sand typically use automated tools. they can include discovery scanning, which attempts to determine the presence of network connected components. network discovery scanning may be combined with port and service identification scanning, which attempts to determine ports, protocols, or services active on network connected systems. vulnerability scanning may also be combined with other scanning techniques; it tests components to identify known vulnerabilities (e.g. missing patch, misconfiguration). various wireless scanning techniques can be applied to detect and evaluate hosts that may have active wireless communications or wireless network connections.

control assessment: control assessment is the most common non adversarial technique applied within many cybersecurity frameworks. defined controls are assessed as either being met, not met, or met to some degree. the following example is from NIST SP 800-53, which is the control catalog that defines the controls, and NIST SP 800-53A, which is the assessment manual that defines how controls are assessed. in NIST SP 800-53A, the assessment procedures break down the original controls from NIST SP 800-53 into granular components that can be assigned to assessment objects (e.g. activities, individuals, mechanisms, specification) and assessed using methods (e.g. test, interview, examine). the following example includes options for all the types of defined assessment objects and assessment methods. not all objects or methods would be used for any given assessment, and part of the plan development includes choosing the most appropriate objects and methods for a particular assessment.


a complex assessment may use thousands of individual method and object asse4ssments to determine the state and effectiveness of controls within the scope of the assessment. 


assessment may occur as often as practicable within the SDLC. they may be executed in parallel with other development testing or functional testing, and the results of early testing may be reused during authorization (in accordance with organizational guidance). the NIST RMF encourages the use of automation in conducting and documenting assessments. some development methodologies (e.g. agile_ may require interactive assessments where individual increments of capability and associated controls are assessed. common controls are typically not assessed with the system, but assessors may still evaluate control applicability and verify effective inheritance. where feasible and in accordance with organizational policy, a certain level of assessment reuse is encourage for efficiency and cost effectiveness, provided that the organization develops criteria for reusing assessment results. in some cases,. external or independent testing programs may provide the results that can be reused and integrated into the assessment. example of external assessment programs includes common criterial evaluation (ISO 15408), NIST Cryptographic Module Validation Program, Payment Card industry Data Security Standard Assessments, and cloud security alliance (CSA) security, trust and assurance registry (STAR) audits


