Verifying and Validating Evidence



during assessments. evidence of cp;;ected with the [rop,aru purpose of veruomg and va;odatomg that a [articular;ar state exists or does not exist.


in this context, evidence can be any artifact from the application of the various assessment methods against various assessment object s and can take many forms (e.g. test results, interview notes, configuration sightings). the purpose of collecting this evidence is to determine whether controls are in place (verified) and accomplishing the intended goal (validated) 


it is possible to achieve verification but fail validation if a control is improperly specified or implemented in a way that meets a specification but fails to achieve a result. some assessments may focus on verification, whereas others focus primarily on validation, but many examine both


verification: verification confirms that specified requirements have been fulfilled. verification asks, "was this built correctly?". this concept assesses whether the defined functional security requirements have been met. have the specifications been met for a subcomponent, component, or the entire system? however, this may not always indicate whether the overall security objectives are met if the specifications or granular requirements were not correct

validation. validation confirms that requirements for a specific intended use have been fulfilled. validation asks, "was the right thing built?". this concept assesses whether the security objectives of confidentiality, integrity, and availability were met with the final design and implementation of the system. it is possible the technical requirements associated with security were met, but has or more weaknesses remain (e.g. unpatched vulnerability, misconfiguration). validation ensures the system effectively meets the security objectives and not just technical requirements or specifications.


verification and validations are system security engineering processes. however, they overlap heavily with security control assessment, and functional verification or validation can easily be combined with security or privacy verification and validation at many stages of the system life cycle if appropriate provisions are made.


integrating these processes not only streamlines the evaluation of a systems security posture but also ensures security and privacy considerations are embedded into the system from the outset. by doing so, organizations can identify potential security vulnerabilities and privacy issues early in the development process, allowing for timely and more cost effective remediation.


