Reviewing the Initial Assessment/Audit Report and Planning Risk Response Actions


once the assessment is complete and at least the interim report has been provided to the organization or system owner, findings from the assessment that represent significant risk may receive prioritization for remediation


some findings may be quickly remediated and reassessed, while others may require long term responses depending on the remediation time frame defined by the organization. in all cases, the original findings are retained, but reports are updated to show correction when they have been reassessed and are no longer deemed as deficiencies. in some cases, addendums that respond to initial assessment reports may be created by the system owner to explain findings or issues; these addendums may also rebut elements of the assessment report if there are disagreements. during remediation activities, system risk assessments may require updates. this process drives initial remediation actions and prioritization as report findings are evaluated for significance and prioritized for action or acceptance. when actions are required, resources are allocated to remediation actions.


assigning risk response based on iden5tified vulnerabilities or deficiencies: immediate remediation concerts involve remediation or corrective action taken just after or during assessment. immediate remediation must follow organizational change control and configuration management processes. systemic changes should not be made during as assessment without following the organizational change control policies or processes. additional testing or assessment activities may be required to ensure the previously identified findings has been corrected and no longer remains a deficiency


standard risk response is used to identify appropriate remediation. these options include: risk avoidance (e.g. stop an action or turn off system functions), risk mitigation (e.g. apply a patch, change a configuration), risk transferring or sharing (e.g. buy insurance), risk acceptance (e.g. request risk acceptance due to minimal risk likelihood or impact of deficiency)


long term or larger remediation needs to go into a long term risk response plan to become follow up activities after the assessment is fully complete.


collaborating with Stakeholders on Risk Response: risk responses can result in changes to process, changes to systems, or retraining of staff. all modifications must be done collaboratively with affected stakeholders. organizations with mature configuration and change management processes can leverage them, but organizations without mature change management processes must manage the changes require by risk responses in a way that ensures stakeholders are not adversely affected. for example, in response to a critical risk identified and associated with inadequate access controls in its customer data management system, an organization might decide to implement multifactor authentication and role base access control (RBAC) as mitigative measures. this initiative necessitates a collaborative approach involving various stakeholders, including IT, human resources, and operations departments, to assess the impact on daily operations and to determine training requirements for staff. organizations equipped with mature configuration and change management processes can seamlessly integrate these changes, conduct thorough impact analyses, define clear communication strategies, and schedule the implementation to minimize disruption..


reassessing and validation non compliant findings with newly applied corrective actions: after risk responses have been identified and applied, it may be necessary for assessors to reassess controls or determine whether the applied corrective actions accomplished the intended goals. this may include reassessing some or all the controls using the same methods and objects in the original assessment but also may include a truncated reassessment based on the assessors judgement. this ensures appropriate levels of independence are maintained, but since the assessor likely has more knowledge of the organization or systems under assessment, they may be able to reasonably accept different evidence of compliance than in the original assessment. for example, the assessor may feel it is unnecessary to return to a location for reassessment of a system after patches are applied. they may simply request a screenshot, remote scan summary, or report from a vulnerability scanner as proof it is patched. however, there are circumstances when the system remains noncompliant, perhaps because the patches were incorrectly applied or they failed to cover all aspects of the vulnerabilities or compatibility issues that lead to different forms of non compliance. because of this, the assessor must be convinced that the solution is sufficient, otherwise, the final report will reflect that issue is an open finding and unresolved. this may then result in negative impacts such as a denial of authority to operate (DATO) under the NIST RMF or the failure to achieve ISO certification


