Developing the Final Assessment/Audit Report



this stage includes the finalization of the assessment or audit reports. once completed, no additional changes will be made, and the assessment activity is complete form the assessors perspective


documenting final compliance: depending on the objectives and type of assessment, assessment items (e.g. controls) are assigned a final status either as observed during the assessment or during a reassessment after risk responses applied. the specific terminology used can vary, but the most typical terms for items that are fully met are "met" or "compliant". for anything not met or non compliant, the assessor determines the degree of compliance (if any) and whether the requirement was applicable. for example, a control may specify that certain wifi settings are applied, but if the scope of the assessment did not include technology with wifi, then the control would not be applicable within the scope of the assessment. additionally, a control determined to be "not met" or "noncompliant" may have been assessed as partially met. most report formats would still designate this control as "not met" or "non compliant" but would require the assessor to document the specific degree to which the control was implemented. while some assessment formats may use only absolutes (e.g. compliant, noncompliant), most formats specify a degree of compliance for anything not completely so.


documenting recommendations when appropriate: most assessment methodologies require the assessor to provide appropriate levels of recommendations to address the risks identified. as previously noted, this does not mean the assessor should necessarily provide detailed technical reengineering services; rather, they should provide actionable general recommendations and leave the specific implementation up to the organization. overly specific recommendations from an external assessor may not consider the specific needs of the organization, and a detailed solution that might work well for one organization may not be feasible. conversely, overly generic recommendations might not provide any useful guidance to resolve an issue or reduce risk. expectations and practical limits for recommendations should be adequately addressed during assessment preparation. crafting recommendations as an assessor involves striking a delicate balance between being too prescriptive and too vague. effective recommendations are those tailed enough to provide a clear direction for action, yet flexible enough to allow the organization to adapt them to it unique circumstances, capabilities, and resource constraints. this approach acknowledges that while external assessors bring valuable expertise and an objective viewpoint, they may not have an intimate understanding of an organizations internal processes, culture, or technical infrastructure


finalizing the assessment report: the assessment report is finalized, and no additional changes are made. this may be a time drive occurrence (due by a certain date) or driven by all stakeholders and the assessors being satisfied with a draft version of the report. the report should be complete, constrain any necessary supporting evidence (often in appendixes), and provide all the necessary information to meet the assessment objectives for all identified stakeholders. the specific contents of the report may vary depending on the objectives of the assessment and framework, but most reports will contain: 

title/identifier: this explicitly identifies this assessment report us9ing title, date, and potentially other unique identifiers

executive summery: the executive summary provides a high level summary of the report contents based on the report objectives. this may include summaries of the major findings if any. 

assessor/auditor name and qualifications: this identifies the assessor or auditor and lists appropriate qualifications to provide confidence that readers of the report competently conducted the assessment. 

findings: this security lists individual findings and the status of any control assessments (e.g. met or not met). for not met items, the assessor will typically detail how the state of the assessed entity did not meet the assessment standard and may include appropriate assessor recommendations for remediation

assessor comments: if appropriate for the assessment type, the assessor may include any general comments that were not covered under the findings section.

organization comments: if appropriate for the assessment type, comments from the organization under assessment may be included. this can include clarifications provided by the organization or not any disagreements with the assessor so that a reviewer of the report sees all perspectives

any other appendices or data appropriate to the assessment: this often includes technical data, automated testing/scanning output, or other artifacts generated during the assessment that could be pertinent for reviewers of the report to access. the specific additional content may vary significantly depending on the assessment framework and purpose of the assessment


a final assessment report may be large with detailed data and evidence. it also often contains information that is sensitive in nature if there are unresolved risks identified in the report. the report may need to have restricted access based on the risks identified within. in the case of external certifications (e.g. ISO certifications), the report may include a publicly shareable summary or certification from the assessor that compliance was achieved or specific goals were met


