Developing a Risk Response Plan



for any risks identified in the assessment report that could not be corrected prior to the final report, the assessed organization will often create a risk response plan


this may reduce or control risks identified in the assessment (e.g. risk management activities) or ensure deficiencies are corrected on a require time line (e.g. compliance activities). since risks that can be addressed quickly or with minimal resources were likely addressed during the initial risk responses between the initial and final reports, the remaining risk items likely require longer time frames or more resources than are immediately available for permanent correction


plan of action and milestone (POA&M): a common term for the long term risk response plan is the plan or action and milestones, which is used by NIST frameworks. other frameworks may simply refer to a generic response plan but contain similar elements to the NIST POA&M. the POA&M describes actions, resource requirements, and milestones to complete tasks associated with remediation. the POA&M is used to monitor progress and provides a record of deficiencies and corrective actions. the organization should maintain a consistent POA&M process that will be used across all departments to help manage organizational risk


POA&Ms should include: weaknesses identified (what is wrong), point of contact (who is responsible), resources required to correct, scheduled completion date for correction, milestones with completion dates, changes to milestones (tracks historical state if plan changes occur), method of identification, and status.


POA&Ms are an extremely valuable tool for the overall organization and can include anything else the organization tracks. when applied at a detailed level, POA&Ms can be created for individual systems and components that better manage resources and provide for budget justifications and resource allocation prioritization since POA&M items are tied to identified risks


detailed POA&Ms should be considered a living record of system status and remediation requirements. 


in many cases, the POA&M may be the only easily accessible record that shows organizational leadership, security status, and various weaknesses associated with the organization or specific systems over time.


identified residual risks and deficiencies: any risk identified in the assessment report that has not had a risk response applied should be included in the POA&M or risk response plan. while some items may not be immediately addressed, or resources may not be available, they should still be recorded to support future prioritization of effort and resource distribution. the response plan can then be used to help track unresolved risk from the organization level down to individual systems and components, depending on the level of detail in the response plans developed by the organization. integrating advanced risk management tools and technologies can further enhance the effectiveness of the POA&M. these tools automate aspects of risk tracking, provide analytical capabilities to assess the potential impacts of risks, and offer visualization features to communicate risk status and trends to stakeholders. leveraging technology in this way can lead to more efficient and effective risk management practices. ultimately, the goal of including all risks in a POA&M or risk response plan, even those not immediately addressed, is to ensure a structured and strategic approach to risk management. this approach helps organizations to9 not only manage their current risk landscape but also build resilience and adaptability into their risk management practices, thus preparing them to respond effectively to future challenges.


prioritizing risk: one of the main values of having a risk response plan is risk prioritization. when used in conjunction with appropriate risk assessments, the response plan includes time lines and resource requirements needed to address risk. this allows the organization to address risk more effectively over time and to better allocate resources. for example, a risk assessment may produce a list of 10 risks with different potential magnitudes based on a risk scoring system. the common vulnerability scoring system (CVSS), for example, produces scores between 1 and 10 for technical risks; other risk frameworks use different risk scoring systems. an organization might use the risk score to rank risks from 1 to 10 based on their importance. that risk ranking can be tied to planned responses that include time lines and resource requirements. the combination of risk assessment and response plan then becomes an important planning tool for the allocation or reallocation of resources to planned responses based on the associated risk


identifying resources and time required to mitigate risk: risk responses require some degree of resources to be applied for some period to reduce risk to an acceptable level. while strictly risk based prioritization helped the organization understand which responses provide the most value, risks must also be weighed against the resources required to adequately address them. for instance, an item that posses a relatively low risk but demands considerable resources to manage should not be prioritized for resource allocation until more critical risks have been addressed. however, it might be in the interest of an organization to address many low risk items that collectively require minimal resources. intelligent resource application is a key part of any risk management strategy and is heavily informed by both risk assessment and response planning that includes time lines and resource requirements. the NIST POA&M format explicitly requires the including of resources and planned time lines for risk mitigation, and NIST control require organizations to track POA&M items over time to ensure they are completed on schedule. ISO standards require similar tracking but are less explicit on what specifically is tracked with respect to each risk


resources required to adequately respond to risk can take many forms, but most tie back to cost (financial resources), availability of skilled staff to implement responses (personnel resources), and available technologies that enable or permit responses (technical resources)


any or all these resource types may be constrained and are rarely in abundance for most organization. the lack of a single critical resource could prevent or delay a risk response even if other resources are available. if an organization has the money (financial) and equipment (technical) to implement a response, but it does not have the personnel to run the equipment, the response may be infeasible

